{
 "turns_text": [
  "[SEG0001] person_1 (PATIENT): Sorry I'm late. The 42 was rammed and I followed the signs to phlebotomy like an idiot.",
  "[SEG0002] person_2 (DOCTOR): You're fine, the slot's still yours. Have a seat.",
  "[SEG0003] person_1 (PATIENT): Cheers. Um, before I forget, my sister rang Tuesday night in a complete flap. They've told her she's got an overactive thyroid, she said Graves, and they've started her on carmbimazole, and she thinks I should be checked because we're built the same and...",
  "[SEG0004] person_2 (DOCTOR): We'll come back to your sister. What actually brought you in today?",
  "[SEG0005] person_1 (PATIENT): I've been losing weight. That's what's worrying me. I'm not dieting, not even trying, and it's coming off anyway.",
  "[SEG0006] person_2 (DOCTOR): How long has that been going on?",
  "[SEG0007] person_1 (PATIENT): Oh it's odd, at the Christmas do I made this enormous lasagne for the nursery, I'm a teaching assistant, and in the photos my manager said my face was fuller then, and I keep going on about that tray of pasta...",
  "[SEG0008] person_2 (DOCTOR): The timing, though.",
  "[SEG0009] person_1 (PATIENT): Sorry. Since about the end of January, so nearly three months, maybe a shade over. I've dropped nearly a stone. I was around 68 kilos back then.",
  "[SEG0010] person_2 (DOCTOR): Has your eating changed at all?",
  "[SEG0011] person_1 (PATIENT): That's the strange bit. I'm eating more than usual. Second helpings, crisps in the staff room, an extra round of toast before work, and the weight still drops. I even finish the kids' leftover fruit at tidy-up, which I never used to.",
  "[SEG0012] person_2 (DOCTOR): Do you get a racing heart?",
  "[SEG0013] person_1 (PATIENT): Yes. Most days, even when I'm just sat marking. It thumps for a few minutes and then it eases off.",
  "[SEG0014] person_2 (DOCTOR): Any chest pain with that?",
  "[SEG0015] person_1 (PATIENT): No. No pain. I did blame the coffee, two mugs before school, but it happens on the days I skip it as well, so...",
  "[SEG0016] person_2 (DOCTOR): Mm. Are you sweating more than you used to?",
  "[SEG0017] person_1 (PATIENT): The boiler at the nursery is a nightmare, we had a proper row with the caretaker in February, the infants' room was freezing and our side was...",
  "[SEG0018] person_1 (PATIENT): Sorry. Yes, I sweat loads, even sitting still, and I wake up a bit damp. Err, when that started I couldn't really tell you, it just crept up.",
  "[SEG0019] person_2 (DOCTOR): Have you had a fever, an actual temperature?",
  "[SEG0020] person_1 (PATIENT): No. I don't think so. Not ill in that way.",
  "[SEG0021] person_2 (DOCTOR): And your hands?",
  "[SEG0022] person_1 (PATIENT): Shaky, both hands. I spill my tea in the staff room, it's embarrassing, and my writing on the board goes wobbly. Umm, I couldn't say when it began, might have been February, might have been later.",
  "[SEG0023] person_2 (DOCTOR): I'll check your pulse and your blood pressure.",
  "[SEG0024] person_1 (PATIENT): Go on then. My mum says I've got my dad's skinny wrists, he was a postman for thirty years, not sure that helps you.",
  "[SEG0025] person_2 (DOCTOR): Pulse is 118, regular. Blood pressure 128 over 76. There's a fine shake in both hands, and your palms are warm and sweaty.",
  "[SEG0026] person_1 (PATIENT): Yeah. I keep telling myself it's stress, Ofsted's looming and I've been wound up for weeks, but eating more and still losing weight doesn't sound like stress. I was reading stuff online at half one, which was probably daft.",
  "[SEG0027] person_2 (DOCTOR): Let me say it back. Nearly three months, nearly a stone down, eating more, heart racing most days, sweating, shaky hands. No chest pain, no fever. And your sister has an overactive thyroid.",
  "[SEG0028] person_1 (PATIENT): Yes, that's the lot. I never brought it to the GP before. Kept putting it off because the afternoon clinic wait is ridiculous and then I'd talk myself out of it on the bus.",
  "[SEG0029] person_2 (DOCTOR): Right. The pattern fits an overactive thyroid best. That's a working impression, not a firm diagnosis. Anxiety can make the heart race, but it doesn't usually cause weight loss while you're eating more.",
  "[SEG0030] person_1 (PATIENT): So it might be the thyroid. Not definite until the tests.",
  "[SEG0031] person_2 (DOCTOR): Exactly. I'm booking blood tests, thyroid function and a full blood count, and an ECG as well.",
  "[SEG0032] person_1 (PATIENT): Do I need to take anything? My sister offered me one of her carmbimazole tablets and I said no, but I wasn't sure if that was sensible or just me being awkward.",
  "[SEG0033] person_2 (DOCTOR): Don't take her tablets. They're not yours, and we shouldn't start anything like that before the bloods.",
  "[SEG0034] person_1 (PATIENT): Fair enough. Uh, will I be alright to keep working? I'm on my feet with the four-year-olds all day and I don't want to keel over in the book corner.",
  "[SEG0035] person_2 (DOCTOR): Keep working as you are for now. Sit down if the heart starts racing, and have a drink of water.",
  "[SEG0036] person_1 (PATIENT): Okay. And after the tests?",
  "[SEG0037] person_2 (DOCTOR): Come back in two weeks and we'll go through the results together."
 ],
